class AbstractNaryRelation {
  definition {
    Abstract base shape for all n-ary concept-system relations:
    PartitiveRelation, GenericRelation, and future
    AssociativeRelation / SequentialRelation.

    All n-ary relations share:
      - one comprehensive concept (the superordinate / genus / frame)
      - two or more members with MECE (presence, count, is_delimiting)
      - completeness (complete | partial)
      - criterion of subdivision (the coherence principle)
      - sources (bibliographic provenance)
      - notes (localized free-form commentary)
      - status (independent lifecycle from the comprehensive concept)

    LUTAML files do not express inheritance — each leaf class
    re-declares these fields with leaf-specific documentation.
    Ruby/JS consumers use real inheritance from an AbstractNaryRelation
    base class. See docs/design/abstract-nary-relation.md.

    Wire field name `members` is uniform across all n-ary relation
    types. The `type` field on the wire object discriminates which
    leaf class to instantiate.

    Per-file storage: each n-ary relation lives in
    `relations/<comprehensive-id>/<criterion-slug>.yaml`. See
    docs/design/relations-as-files.md.
  }
  +comprehensive: <<StandardDocument>> ReferenceToTermbase [1] {
    definition {
      The superordinate concept of the rake.

      For PartitiveRelation: the whole (superordinate concept partitive).
      For GenericRelation: the genus (superordinate concept generic).
      For future AssociativeRelation: the frame concept.

      Always encoded as a ConceptRef (source + id, optional text).
      May reference an ExternalConcept (status: external) when the
      comprehensive is a parenthetical term defined elsewhere.
    }
  }
  +members: ConceptSystemMember [2..*] {
    definition {
      Two or more members of the rake. ISO 704 requires "two or more";
      a single pairwise assertion should be expressed as a binary
      edge (has_part, broader_generic, related), not an n-ary relation.

      Each member carries ISO 704:2022 MECE dimensions: presence,
      count, is_delimiting. See ConceptSystemMember.

      Leaf classes constrain the member type (PartitiveMember for
      PartitiveRelation, GenericMember for GenericRelation).
    }
  }
  +completeness: Completeness [0..1] {
    definition {
      Whether the encoded members fitted together constitute the
      comprehensive (complete) or only some of it (partial).

      Defaults to complete when omitted.
    }
  }
  +criterion: <<BasicDocument>> LocalizedString [0..1] {
    definition {
      The principle by which the comprehensive is divided into members.
      ISO 12620 coordinate-concept coherence: members within one
      relation share the comprehensive AND share the criterion.

      Two n-ary relations on the same comprehensive with the same
      criterion are duplicates (validation error). Two with different
      criteria are distinct decompositions.

      Multilingual (relation-level metadata, lives with the relation
      not the comprehensive). Required when the comprehensive has
      more than one relation (otherwise distinctness cannot be verified).
    }
  }
  +sources: ConceptSource [0..*] {
    definition {
      Bibliographic references for this specific relation. Independent
      of the comprehensive concept's own sources.

      Use case: a rake is introduced by a specific source (e.g., OIML
      V 2-200:2010 §5.1 Figure 1) that may differ from the sources of
      the comprehensive concept itself.
    }
  }
  +notes: <<BasicDocument>> LocalizedString [0..1] {
    definition {
      Free-form localized commentary on this relation. Independent of
      the comprehensive concept's notes.

      Use case: explaining why a particular decomposition was chosen,
      or noting editorial decisions about which species were included.
    }
  }
  +status: ConceptStatus [0..1] {
    definition {
      Lifecycle status of this relation, independent of the comprehensive
      concept's status. Defaults to the comprehensive's status when omitted.

      Use case: a comprehensive concept is `valid` while a rake
      decomposition is `draft` (newly proposed) — the relation file's
      status reflects this.
    }
  }
}
